<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Swedish Livonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1629 CE – 1721 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Livonia under the Swedish crown, home to Riga, one of the largest cities of the Swedish realm, and to the university founded at Dorpat in 1632.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustav II Adolf</a> captured Riga from Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1621, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-altmark">Truce of Altmark</a> in 1629 confirmed Swedish possession of most of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a>, including southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> and the island of Ösel after 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> in the south-east stayed Polish.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Swedish governor-general ruled from Riga, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic German</a> knighthood kept its diet and Lutheran church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustav II Adolf</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Dorpat</a> in 1632, and the pastor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-gluck">Ernst Glück</a> translated the Bible into Latvian in 1685-1689. Riga's exports of grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> made it one of the busiest ports of the Swedish empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI's</a> reduction of noble estates provoked the opposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-reinhold-patkul">Johann Reinhold Patkul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> Russian troops besieged and took Riga in 1710 after a plague-stricken defence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where it became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-livonia">Governorate of Livonia</a>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> remembered the Swedish period as 'the good Swedish times'.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swedish-livonia.jpg" alt="Swedish Livonia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: User: David Newton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_ensign_of_Sweden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1629 CE – 1721 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gustav II Adolf, Charles XI, Charles XII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian, Estonian, Swedish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Latvia, Estonia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Livonia (Swedish), Svenska Livland</dd></dl></aside></div>
